  "Martial Artist": [
    {
      "label": "Adept",
      "_note": "jane-fawn is the corpus model, and the defensive shape is hers: plain PD/ED with almost nothing resistant (she runs PD 2 / rDEF 3). A martial artist's defence is not being where the punch lands, so Resistant Protection here is 'Combat Luck' and nothing more. Built with a 60-point Resistant Protection first, it scored +8.6% — the Rule of X compounds dAP against DCV x DEF x rDEF, and a force field on a DCV 9 character is worth +8 on its own.\n\nThe maneuvers are NOT powers — a .hdc keeps them in their own bucket — but their cost comes out of the powers balance, exactly as the 5E prose folds them into powersCost. STR 18 only punches for DC 3; Offensive Strike (+4) and five Extra DCs carry it to the campaign's DC 12, because a martial artist's damage is technique rather than muscle.\n\nAuthoring a maneuver needs four fields HD writes into a .hdc and a template does not supply on its own: dc/ocv/dcv, `effect` (the engine gates its damage roll on hasOwn(trait,'effect')), `category`, and `addstr` (Y for Martial/Offensive Strike, N for Nerve Strike — an NND is technique, not muscle). Miss `effect` and the maneuver rolls nothing; miss `addstr` and it loses STR silently.\n\nNo Lightning Reflexes here, though a martial artist wants it: it is a TALENT, and a powerset carries only powers and maneuvers. It belongs in the skillset — and Phil's sheet counts it toward DEX, which ruleOfXStats reads from character.talents.",
